---
import { Picture } from "astro:assets"
import CaseStudy from "@/layouts/case-study.astro"
import AspectRatio from "@/components/ui/aspect-ratio.astro"
import { getProject } from "@/data/projects"
import grouchWhite from "@/assets/projects/grouch/grouchwhite.png"
import hat from "@/assets/projects/grouch/hat.jpg"
import setup from "@/assets/projects/grouch/setup.jpg"
import stickers from "@/assets/projects/grouch/stickers.jpg"

const project = getProject("grouch")!
const pictureFormats: ("avif" | "webp")[] = ["avif", "webp"]
---

<CaseStudy
  project={project}
  title="Grouch"
  description="Brand identity, apparel graphics, and small-run production for a limited merch experiment."
  seoTitle="Grouch · Brand and Apparel Work"
  seoDescription="Grouch was a limited-run brand and apparel project by Noah Michaels, spanning identity, T-shirts, hats, totes, stickers, and hands-on production."
>
  <h2>Overview</h2>
  <p>
    Grouch was a small apparel identity built around a rough-edged wordmark, a
    reworked public-domain cartoon, and a limited merch run.
  </p>
  <p>
    I carried the artwork across screen-printed shirts, embroidered hats, totes,
    and stickers, keeping the system deliberately constrained so production
    stayed consistent.
  </p>

  <h2>Brand</h2>
  <p>
    The source art is a public-domain Felix the Cat still. I ran it through a
    filter and reworked the result, then drew the wordmark to sit on top of it.
    The blown-out, high-contrast texture that came out of that process is what
    gave the graphic its bootleg-flyer character, and it separates cleanly for
    screen printing, which fine-lined artwork would not have.
  </p>
  <p>
    The identity stayed simple enough for a shirt, bold enough to hold its own
    as a sticker, and flexible enough for embroidery. The same artwork ran in
    two colorways: black only, and black plus a red-orange hit on the wordmark.
  </p>

  <figure>
    <AspectRatio ratio={4 / 3}>
      <Picture
        src={grouchWhite}
        formats={pictureFormats}
        widths={[600, 1000]}
        sizes="(min-width: 768px) 768px, 100vw"
        alt="White Grouch T-shirt printed in black only, with the wordmark above a cartoon-cat graphic."
      />
    </AspectRatio>
    <figcaption>
      The black-only colorway, printed from the same artwork as the two-color
      version.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>Production</h2>
  <p>
    The project moved from identity into physical objects. Shirts were screen
    printed by hand, while hats and stickers were produced offsite. A small line
    kept the system manageable and gave each piece a clear role.
  </p>

  <div class="grid gap-8 sm:grid-cols-2">
    <figure>
      <AspectRatio ratio={4 / 3}>
        <Picture
          src={hat}
          formats={pictureFormats}
          widths={[480, 800]}
          sizes="(min-width: 768px) 360px, 100vw"
          alt="Black Grouch snapback hat with an embroidered orange logo."
        />
      </AspectRatio>
      <figcaption>Snapback hat with embroidered Grouch logo.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure>
      <AspectRatio ratio={4 / 3}>
        <Picture
          src={stickers}
          formats={pictureFormats}
          widths={[480, 800]}
          sizes="(min-width: 768px) 360px, 100vw"
          alt="Die-cut Grouch stickers arranged on a tabletop."
        />
      </AspectRatio>
      <figcaption>Die-cut stickers carried the mark beyond apparel.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <figure>
    <AspectRatio ratio={4 / 3}>
      <Picture
        src={setup}
        formats={pictureFormats}
        widths={[720, 1200]}
        sizes="(min-width: 768px) 768px, 100vw"
        alt="Screen-printing press with the Grouch graphic inked on the screen, mid-print."
      />
    </AspectRatio>
    <figcaption>
      Every shirt was pulled by hand on a single-station press.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>
</CaseStudy>
